Ladybird accepts prefixed radial gradients with an optional leading
position, a shape and extent or two radii, and legacy sRGB color
interpolation. It maps “cover” to farthest-corner and “contain” to
closest-side. Blink uses ConsumeDeprecatedRadialGradient(), while
WebKit uses consumePrefixedRadialGradient(). Gecko handles this in
Gradient::parse_radial() and EndingShape::parse(), and maps aliases
in ShapeExtent::parse_with_compat_mode() to the same geometry.

Ladybird preserves the prefix and extent spelling when serializing.
Blink does this in CSSRadialGradientValue::CustomCSSText(). WebKit
preserves aliases in Serialize<PrefixedRadialGradient::Ellipse> and
Serialize<PrefixedRadialGradient::Circle>, but omits an ellipse with no
explicit size. Gecko canonicalizes aliases and omits defaults in its
Gradient::to_css(). Ladybird follows Blink for these strings, including
the default “cover” extent when the shape is explicit.

Ladybird requires commas after explicit positions and sizes, matching
WebKit, Gecko, and the compatibility WPTs. Blink also accepts some
missing commas. Ladybird accepts a single color stop and expands a
double-position stop into two stops, matching Blink and WebKit. Hints
and interpolation-method syntax are rejected, and colors interpolate
in sRGB even when their syntax is modern.
